From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: give the kernel its crypto back - this one is a boot stop

Found while working the guard inventory, and it outranks everything else on
the list because it is not a missing feature: on milanf today the first
`SHA256_Init()` in the kernel dereferences a NULL function table.

`libkern/crypto/register_crypto.c` defines

    SECURITY_READ_ONLY_LATE(crypto_functions_t) g_crypto_funcs = NULL;

and `crypto_register_functions()` is the only writer. On Apple hardware the
corecrypto kext calls it. There is no such kext here, so on this port
`g_crypto_funcs` is NULL for the life of the kernel.

The sweet port dealt with that by giving the five legacy digest/cipher
entry points their own portable implementations, chosen at compile time:

    corecrypto_sha2.c   SHA-256 -> ccdigest_init(&ccsha256_ltc_di, ...)
                        SHA-384/512 -> BearSSL (sweet_sha2big.c)
    corecrypto_sha1.c   -> BearSSL (sweet_sha1.c)
    corecrypto_md5.c    -> BearSSL (sweet_md5.c)
    corecrypto_aes.c    -> BearSSL bitslice AES (sweet_aes.c)

Every one of those choices sits in a `#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)`
arm with the corecrypto call as the `#else`. So on milanf the file compiles,
the `#else` arm is taken, and:

| call | site | result on milanf, today |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SHA256_Init/Update/Final` | `bsd/kern/kern_exec.c:6046` | NULL deref on the first exec |
| `SHA256_Init` | `osfmk/vm/vm_kern.c:4066` | NULL deref in the address hash |
| `SHA256_Init` | `osfmk/ipc/ipc_kmsg.c:551` | NULL deref on kmsg scrubbing |
| `SHA256_*` | `bsd/kern/chunklist.c:251/390` | NULL deref |
| `MD5_*` | `bsd/net/pf.c:4464`, `bsd/netinet/tcp_subr.c:2772` | NULL deref |
| `SHA1_*` | `bsd/netinet6/in6_ifattach.c:133`, `iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp:2193` | NULL deref |
| `aes_encrypt_key` | `bsd/netinet6/esp_rijndael.c:111` | NULL deref |

`kern_exec.c:6046` is the one that matters: it is the bootsession-UUID/CDHash
digest on the exec path, so the first `execve` of launchd takes the machine
down. `osfmk/prng/entropy.c` was already widened by an earlier patch and calls
`ccdigest_init(&ccsha256_ltc_di, ...)` directly, which is why the very earliest
boot does not fall over - the hole opens at the first userspace exec, which is
far enough in to look like an unrelated crash.

The fix is the two-dozen-line mechanical one, and it is mechanical precisely
because the sweet port already did the work: widen the arm so MILANF takes the
portable path too. Nothing new is written, no value is read off the DTB, and
there is no behavioural difference between the two boards here - neither has a
corecrypto kext, so both want the same answer.

What the 15 `sweet_*.c` files are, since the names are misleading: each is a
four-line translation unit that `#include`s one staged BearSSL source file.
They exist as separate TUs so the build system can compile them under
`optional crypto` without dragging BearSSL into a single blob, and they are
already listed in `libkern/conf/files` with no board condition. The staging
script (`0020-stage-bearssl.sh`) already selects on `SWEET|MILANF`, so the
sources are fetched for a milanf build today; only the per-file gates were
keeping them out. That means this patch adds no build-time dependency and no
new CI step.

Verified without a compiler: apply --check exit 0, then a real apply to the
ci-tail scratch tree - nineteen files cleanly (twenty gates: `corecrypto_sha2.c`
has two), CR=0 in sources and patch. Link unverified; CI in the morning.
`crypto` and `CRYPTO_SHA2` are unconditional in `config/MASTER` (L173/L174), so
the `optional crypto` files are compiled for milanf regardless of
MASTER.arm64.milanf's option list.

diff --git a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_aes.c b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_aes.c
index 384abb6..60214fd 100644
--- a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_aes.c
+++ b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_aes.c
@@ -32,8 +32,12 @@
 #include <corecrypto/ccaes.h>
 #include <kern/debug.h>
 
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
-/* SWEET has a built-in portable AES provider for the legacy CBC/ECB API. */
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
+/*
+ * These boards have a built-in portable AES provider for the legacy
+ * CBC/ECB API (sweet_aes.c). There is no corecrypto kext, so
+ * g_crypto_funcs stays NULL and this is the only working arm.
+ */
 extern const struct ccmode_cbc sweet_aes_cbc_encrypt;
 extern const struct ccmode_cbc sweet_aes_cbc_decrypt;
 #define AES_CBC_ENCRYPT_MODE (&sweet_aes_cbc_encrypt)
diff --git a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_md5.c b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_md5.c
index e9bbc42..70405f6 100644
--- a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_md5.c
+++ b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_md5.c
@@ -17,12 +17,13 @@ setCount(MD5_CTX *ctx, uint64_t count)
 }
 
 /* Copy a ccdigest ctx into a legacy MD5 context */
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 /*
- * SWEET has no corecrypto kext (g_crypto_funcs stays NULL). MD5 uses
- * BearSSL's portable implementation (libkern/crypto/sweet_md5.c), with
- * MD5_CTX carrying exactly its state: chaining value, byte count and the
- * partial block, converted around each call like the corecrypto path does.
+ * These boards have no corecrypto kext (g_crypto_funcs stays NULL).
+ * MD5 uses BearSSL's portable implementation
+ * (libkern/crypto/sweet_md5.c), with MD5_CTX carrying exactly its state:
+ * chaining value, byte count and the partial block, converted around
+ * each call like the corecrypto path does.
  */
 #include "sweet_bearssl/inc/bearssl_hash.h"
 
@@ -71,7 +72,7 @@ MD5Final(unsigned char digest[MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH], MD5_CTX *ctx)
 	br_md5_out(&br_ctx, digest);
 }
 
-#else /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
+#else /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET && !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF */
 
 static void
 DiToMD5(const struct ccdigest_info *di, struct ccdigest_ctx *di_ctx, MD5_CTX *md5_ctx)
@@ -125,4 +126,4 @@ MD5Final(unsigned char digest[MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH], MD5_CTX *ctx)
 	ccdigest_final(di, di_ctx, digest);
 }
 
-#endif /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
+#endif /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET && !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF */
diff --git a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha1.c b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha1.c
index 409533d..65224c1 100644
--- a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha1.c
+++ b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha1.c
@@ -16,12 +16,13 @@ setCount(SHA1_CTX *ctx, uint64_t count)
 	ctx->c.b64[0] = count;
 }
 
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 /*
- * SWEET has no corecrypto kext (g_crypto_funcs stays NULL). SHA-1 uses
- * BearSSL's portable implementation (libkern/crypto/sweet_sha1.c), with
- * SHA1_CTX carrying exactly its state: chaining value, byte count and the
- * partial block, converted around each call like the corecrypto path does.
+ * These boards have no corecrypto kext (g_crypto_funcs stays NULL).
+ * SHA-1 uses BearSSL's portable implementation
+ * (libkern/crypto/sweet_sha1.c), with SHA1_CTX carrying exactly its state:
+ * chaining value, byte count and the partial block, converted around
+ * each call like the corecrypto path does.
  */
 #include "sweet_bearssl/inc/bearssl_hash.h"
 
@@ -70,7 +71,7 @@ SHA1Final(void *digest, SHA1_CTX *ctx)
 	br_sha1_out(&br_ctx, digest);
 }
 
-#else /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
+#else /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET && !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF */
 
 /* Copy a ccdigest ctx into a legacy SHA1 context */
 static void
@@ -125,7 +126,7 @@ SHA1Final(void *digest, SHA1_CTX *ctx)
 	ccdigest_final(di, di_ctx, digest);
 }
 
-#endif /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
+#endif /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET && !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF */
 
 /* This is not publicised in header, but exported in libkern.exports */
 void SHA1Final_r(SHA1_CTX *context, void *digest);
diff --git a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha2.c b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha2.c
index c954dd2..e5a3d79 100644
--- a/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha2.c
+++ b/libkern/crypto/corecrypto_sha2.c
@@ -34,13 +34,13 @@
 
 #if defined(CRYPTO_SHA2)
 
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 /*
- * SWEET has no corecrypto kext, so g_crypto_funcs stays NULL. SHA-256 uses
- * XNU's built-in implementation directly, as the SWEET PRNG does; this also
- * serves code-signing page hashes. SHA-384/512 use BearSSL's portable
- * implementation (libkern/crypto/sweet_sha2big.c), its context stored in
- * the caller's SHA384_CTX/SHA512_CTX.
+ * These boards have no corecrypto kext, so g_crypto_funcs stays NULL.
+ * SHA-256 uses XNU's built-in implementation directly, as the PRNG does;
+ * this also serves code-signing page hashes. SHA-384/512 use BearSSL's
+ * portable implementation (libkern/crypto/sweet_sha2big.c), its context
+ * stored in the caller's SHA384_CTX/SHA512_CTX.
  */
 #include <corecrypto/ccsha2.h>
 #define SHA256_DI()                     (&ccsha256_ltc_di)
@@ -84,7 +84,7 @@ SHA256_Final(void *digest, SHA256_CTX *ctx)
 	ccdigest_final(di, ctx->ctx, digest);
 }
 
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 #include "sweet_bearssl/inc/bearssl_hash.h"
 
 _Static_assert(sizeof(br_sha384_context) <= sizeof(SHA512_CTX),
@@ -127,7 +127,7 @@ SHA512_Final(void *digest, SHA512_CTX *ctx)
 	br_sha512_out(BR_CTX(ctx), digest);
 }
 
-#else /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
+#else /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET && !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF */
 
 void
 SHA384_Init(SHA384_CTX *ctx)
@@ -194,7 +194,7 @@ SHA512_Final(void *digest, SHA512_CTX *ctx)
 	ccdigest_final(di, ctx->ctx, digest);
 }
 
-#endif /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
+#endif /* !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET && !ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF */
 
 #else
 
diff --git a/libkern/crypto/sweet_aes.c b/libkern/crypto/sweet_aes.c
index 21be4eb..a1a0b97 100644
--- a/libkern/crypto/sweet_aes.c
+++ b/libkern/crypto/sweet_aes.c
@@ -1,10 +1,11 @@
 /*
- * SWEET legacy AES CBC/ECB provider backed by BearSSL's portable bitslice AES.
+ * Legacy AES CBC/ECB provider for the Qualcomm boards (sweet, milanf),
+ * backed by BearSSL's portable bitslice AES.
  * Upstream sources and MIT license are staged by 0020-stage-bearssl.sh.
  * No heap, SIMD or CPU crypto extension is needed. Contexts can be copied:
  * their only pointer is the immutable BearSSL class descriptor.
  */
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 #include <libkern/libkern.h>
 #include <libkern/crypto/aes.h>
 #include <corecrypto/ccmode.h>
@@ -108,4 +109,4 @@ const struct ccmode_cbc sweet_aes_cbc_decrypt = {
     .init = sweet_aes_decrypt_init,
     .cbc = sweet_aes_decrypt_cbc,
 };
-#endif /* ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
+#endif /* ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET || ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF */
